syntax:
template< class RandomIt >
void stable_sort( RandomIt first, RandomIt last );

// Overload with comparator:
template< class RandomIt, class Compare >
void stable_sort( RandomIt first, RandomIt last, Compare comp );

Implementation notes: The function requires random access iterators for efficiency. The standard library implementation often uses a top-down mergesort, which recursively splits the range and merges subranges using a temporary array. This ensures stability by merging in a way that preserves the order of equal elements, but it requires additional memory allocation, which can be a performance bottleneck in low-memory environments.

code_example:
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> coll = {5, 3, 4, 3, 2, 1};  // Duplicate elements to demonstrate stability
    std::stable_sort(coll.begin(), coll.end());
    for (int elem : coll) {
        std::cout << elem << ' ';  // Output: 1 2 3 3 4 5 (original order of equal elements preserved)
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
